sophie (android): quick repro: onboarding sample app upload cancel button doesn't stop the upload. spinner stuck, network bytes keep flowing. Pixel 7A Android 13 & Pixel 8 Android 14.

mike (sdk): is the cancel button calling Call.cancel() or are you doing something custom?

sophie (android): we call call.cancel() from UI thread. UI spinner stops getting progress updates after cancel, but traffic continues to the device / carrier for ~10s. battery/network spike reported by QA.

anna: are you using a streaming RequestBody (reading file directly) or buffering to byte[]?

sophie (android): streaming RequestBody to avoid copying big images. simplified body: 

```
RequestBody create(final File f) {
  return new RequestBody() {
    @Override public MediaType contentType() { return MediaType.get("image/jpeg"); }
    @Override public void writeTo(BufferedSink sink) throws IOException {
      InputStream in = new FileInputStream(f);
      byte[] buf = new byte[8192];
      int read;
      while ((read = in.read(buf)) != -1) {
        sink.write(buf, 0, read);
        progressCallback.onProgress(read);
      }
      in.close();
    }
  };
}
```

mike (sdk): classic. OkHttp's Call.cancel() marks the call and closes the socket, but if writeTo is blocked in the native send path or the InputStream is slow it might keep sending. Also if progressCallback posts at front of queue, UI might stop seeing updates but bytes still flowing. Do you use okio.timeout() anywhere?

sophie (android): no. we rely on cancel(). we also observed `java.io.IOException: cancelled` only after several seconds.

anna: two practical fixes: 1) make RequestBody cooperative: check Thread.currentThread().isInterrupted() or a cancellation token between reads and throw IOException to abort quickly. 2) use Okio timeout on the sink: `sink.timeout().throwIfReached()` patterns or call.timeout().cancel() from cancel path.

julian (eng-runtime): addendum: Call.cancel() will try to close the connection but not forcibly interrupt a blocked native send on some devices. On Android, closing the underlying FileDescriptor may only happen when the system schedules it. Safer approach: keep a shared AtomicBoolean cancelled flag, check it in writeTo and also close the InputStream from UI cancel handler (closing source will unblock read). Example: `AtomicBoolean cancelled; public void cancel() { cancelled.set(true); source.close(); }`

mike (sdk): for coroutines: use suspendCancellableCoroutine and register cancellation handler that calls call.cancel() and also toggles the stream/flag. Our Retrofit coroutine adapter sample does call.cancel() only; we should add stream-close too. Also add example using `call.timeout().cancel()` before network heavy ops to propagate.

sophie (android): here's a patched RequestBody I used locally that fixed repro: 

```
RequestBody streaming(File f, AtomicBoolean cancelled) {
  return new RequestBody() {
    @Override public void writeTo(BufferedSink sink) throws IOException {
      try (Source src = Okio.source(f)) {
        Buffer buf = new Buffer();
        while (true) {
          long r = src.read(buf, 8192);
          if (r == -1) break;
          if (cancelled.get() || Thread.currentThread().isInterrupted()) throw new IOException("cancelled by app");
          sink.write(buf, r);
          sink.flush();
        }
      }
    }
  };
}
```

anna: nice. also recommend not posting progress with `postAtFrontOfQueue`, use `post()` and coalesce small updates to avoid main thread churn.

mike (sdk): action items: devex add a cancellable streaming sample to onboarding app + coroutine example with cancellation handler; add a short note in SDK docs about using separate auth client if uploads + refreshes compete for sockets (see earlier thread). write a small integration test that cancels mid-upload and asserts sockets closed.

ci-bot: added flaky e2e test to pipeline (branch: fix/stream-cancel-216543). build queued.

julian (eng-runtime): FYI we should also document that some devices have kernel-level behavior where socket closure is delayed; thus app-layer cooperative cancellation is always more reliable than relying purely on Call.cancel().

sophie (android): thanks all, i'll push the sample and ping for review.
